  "topic": "Error Checking for Function Return Values",
  "explanation": "Error checking for function return values in C involves verifying if a function's return indicates failure, such as -1 for many system calls, and handling it to prevent crashes or undefined behavior. It's used to make programs robust, ensure reliable execution, and provide meaningful error messages to users or logs.",
  "syntax": "if (function_call == error_value) error_handler_function();",
  "code_example": [
    "#include <time.h>",
    "#include <stdio.h>",
    "#include <stdlib.h>",
    "int main() {",
    "    time_t result;",
    "    if ((result = time(NULL)) == (time_t)-1) {",
    "        perror(\"time error\");",
    "        return 1;",
    "    }",
    "    printf(\"Time retrieved successfully: %ld\\n\", result);",
    "    return 0;",
    "}"
  ],
  "example_explanation": "This example demonstrates error checking by calling the time() function and verifying if it returns (time_t)-1, which indicates an error. If an error occurs, it uses perror() to print an error message and exits with a non-zero status, showing how to handle failures gracefully while keeping the program simple and focused on this concept."
}
